




:: warrior_queen_day_4_1
<<set $warrior_queen_arc_step = 4>>
/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
DAY 4   ARCHERY REIMAGINED
Part 1: The Iron Garden   Morning Drill
═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

The rapier bites the air where Aldric's throat had been. He is already gone, pivoted three inches to the right with the languid economy of a man who could dodge death between heartbeats and still have time to yawn, and his practice blade comes sweeping laterally toward my exposed ribs.

I cross-step. Left foot over right, hips turning, the feminine guard carrying me out of the line as smoothly as a boat sliding from a dock. The blade passes through the space I have just vacated, and I riposte, the rapier snaking forward from the seconda guard, point reaching for the gap between his arm and torso.

He parries. Barely. The flat of his practice blade bats the rapier aside a half-second before the point would have touched his ribs.

<<speech "aldric">>Faster. Your second step is still dragging.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>My second step is //tired//. Every part of me is tired. My second step is considering mutiny.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Tell your second step that mutiny is punishable by additional drills.<</speech>>

We have been at it since before dawn, an hour of drill that began with the feminine guard stance-hold and progressed into the three-guard sequence Aldric introduced yesterday. Prima, seconda, terza. We transition between them smoothly, without resetting. Each guard flows into the next like stanzas of a violent poem.

The training corset holds me firm beneath the leathers. I laced it myself this morning, still poorly, the ties slightly uneven, the left cup sitting a fraction higher than the right, but the improvement over Maera's binding is categorical. The breasts, which spent yesterday bouncing during the rapier drills with enough enthusiasm to entertain the soldiers on the rampart, are compressed, cradled, and //silent//. No bouncing. No shifting. The small mounds of tender flesh sit in their padded cups like passengers in a carriage, aware of the journey but insulated from the road.

The nipples, however, have opinions about the morning chill.

They hardened the instant I stepped into the Iron Garden's pre-dawn cold, puckering beneath the corset's padding into stiff peaks that press outward against the lambskin lining with a persistence that is becoming deeply familiar. The padding mutes the sensation, thank every god for Veletia's attention to that particular detail, but I can still //feel// them. Two points of heightened awareness on a chest that is becoming, day by day, a landscape of increasingly complex sensation.

I lunge. Prima guard, blade high, off-hand raised behind me like a dancer's counterweight. The thrust is clean, driven from the hips, the new centre of gravity pulling the motion downward and //forward// in a way the old, shoulder-driven lunge never had. The rapier's point stops an inch from Aldric's breastplate.

He looks down at it.

<<speech "aldric">>That will do.<</speech>>

From Aldric, //that will do// is a standing ovation.

[[Continue the morning drill|warrior_queen_day_4_2]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_2

We drill the three-guard transition for another twenty minutes. I am drenched, sweat running in rivers down my spine, soaking the linen beneath the corset, darkening the leather of the tunic. The corset itself is damp, the lambskin lining warm against breast skin flushed with exertion. Each breath pulls the boning tight across my ribs and then releases it, a rhythmic compression and expansion that the growing tissue has started to... accommodate. Not enjoy, precisely. But accept. The way one accepts the rhythm of a heartbeat, present, persistent, eventually invisible.

The sand sack is absent today. Aldric decided, after yesterday's session, that the corset alone provides sufficient simulation for the current stage of breast development. The sand sack will return, he warns, as the breasts grow, as the weight increases beyond what the natural tissue currently provides. For now, my own small swells, compressed and cradled in the cups, are enough.

//Enough.// As though having breasts at all, having flesh on my chest that bounces and aches and hardens in the cold and responds to touch with a sensitivity that still startles me every time I apply the salve, were a matter of //sufficient weight// rather than a complete overhaul of every assumption I have ever held about the body I live in.

<<speech "aldric">>Hold there.<</speech>>

I freeze mid-transition, rapier halfway between seconda and terza. Sweat drips from my chin. The braid has loosened, strands of hair falling across my face, catching on my eyelashes. I blow them aside with a puff of breath that comes out higher-pitched than intended.

Aldric sheathes his practice blade and turns toward the yard entrance.

<<speech "aldric">>There is someone I wish you to meet.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Who is it?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Someone who understands a problem you do not yet know you have.<</speech>>

He walks toward the gate. I stand in the Iron Garden's centre, rapier in hand, sweat cooling on skin softer than it should be, and watch him go. The sun climbs the eastern wall. The mist burns off. Somewhere in the castle, a bell tolls the morning hour.

I have approximately thirty seconds of peace before the most terrifying woman I have ever met walks into the training yard.

[[Dame Yseldra arrives|warrior_queen_day_4_3]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_3

Dame Yseldra Thornehart enters the Iron Garden as though she were born from its cobblestones, fully formed, fully armoured, and fully unimpressed with everything she surveys.

She is tall. Taller than Aldric, which is itself a feat requiring either exceptional genetics or a pact with gods of unusual generosity. Her braided blonde hair is imprisoned beneath a steel circlet that catches the morning light and throws it back with interest. Her spear is absent, but a recurve bow rides in her left hand with the easy lethality that other women reserve for a fan or a flower. She wears half-plate over boiled leather, and every piece of it is immaculate, oiled, maintained, and arranged with the precision of a woman who considers imperfection a personal insult.

She regards me with ice-blue eyes that weigh, measure, and find me wanting. All in the space of a single heartbeat.

<<speech "yseldra">>So. The prince who would be queen.<</speech>>

I straighten. The rapier is still in my hand. The sweat is still on my face. The breasts, beneath the corset and the leathers, are still //there//. I cannot command their absence any more than I could command the tide.

<<speech "mc">>The heir. Who //shall// be queen.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Bold words for someone whose braid is falling apart.<</speech>>

I reach back. She is right, the braid has loosened into a tangled suggestion of organisation, strands escaping in every direction. I tug the leather tie free, gather the mass, heavier, thicker, longer than it was even a week ago, and retie it with fingers that fumble.

Aldric reappears beside Yseldra. He has materialised silently, as is his deeply unfair custom.

<<speech "aldric">>Dame Yseldra Thornehart. Inner Court Sentinel. The finest archer in Velmoria. Possibly the finest in the realm.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Possibly.<</speech>>

She says it without modesty or boast, a simple acknowledgment of probability, like a mathematician stating an axiom.

<<speech "aldric">>She will teach you the bow. I shall return at sundown.<</speech>>

He departs as though the matter were routine. Which, for Aldric, it likely is. The man delegates with the same efficiency he employs in ending lives, quickly, cleanly, and without discussion.

I am alone with Dame Yseldra Thornehart.

The temperature in the Iron Garden drops approximately five degrees, which I attribute to her presence rather than any meteorological event.

[[Yseldra's assessment|warrior_queen_day_4_4]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_4

She circles me. Slowly. With the same patient, predatory orbit that Aldric employs, but where Aldric circles like a wolf assessing prey, Yseldra circles like a hawk, high, detached, seeing everything from an altitude that renders the subject small and entirely knowable.

<<speech "yseldra">>Turn.<</speech>>

I turn. Present my back.

<<speech "yseldra">>Arms out.<</speech>>

I extend my arms. The posture stretches the training corset across my chest, the boning pulling slightly, the cups adjusting to the new angle. I can feel the breast tissue shift within the cups, a small lateral migration as the arms extend, drawing the pectoral muscle outward and carrying the attached tissue with it. A movement I would not have noticed a month ago. A movement I now catalogue automatically, filed under the growing library of //things the breasts do//.

<<speech "yseldra">>Hm. Noted.<</speech>>

She completes the circuit and stands before me. Her eyes, pale blue, the colour of winter ice at its thinnest, track from my shoulders to my hands to my chest to my hips with the detached precision of an archer measuring a target.

<<speech "yseldra">>Your shoulders have narrowed. Two inches, perhaps more, since last I saw you at the harvest tournament. The deltoids have thinned. The biceps are leaner. The forearms are...<</speech>>

she seizes my right forearm without permission, turns it, examines the underside

<<speech "yseldra">>...still strong, but the wrist joint has loosened. More flexibility. Less rigidity. Good for the rapier. Problematic for the draw.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>What draw?<</speech>>

She releases my arm and walks toward the weapons rack at the yard's edge. From it, she lifts a recurve bow, not the massive seventy-pound war bow I trained with since seventeen, but something lighter. A hunting bow, perhaps, or a cavalry bow. The limbs are shorter, the grip narrower, the draw weight... lower.

<<speech "yseldra">>Fifty pounds,<</speech>>

she says, answering the question I had not asked.

<<speech "yseldra">>Your old bow was seventy. That draw weight was calibrated for broad shoulders, thick deltoids, and a flat chest that offered the string an unobstructed path from hand to anchor. You no longer possess any of those things.<</speech>>

The observation lands like a slap delivered with surgical precision.

<<speech "yseldra">>The shoulders cannot sustain a seventy-pound draw at full extension. Not anymore. Not with the muscle you have lost and the flexibility you have gained. We begin at fifty and rebuild from there. Take it.<</speech>>

[[Take the bow|warrior_queen_day_4_5]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_5

I take the bow. It is lighter than my old one, significantly so. The grip is narrower, designed for a hand that is itself becoming narrower, the fingers wrapping around the leather with a fit that is... better than the old bow provided in weeks. My palm has thinned. The old grip had been sitting in a hand that was slowly outgrowing it, or rather, //ingrowing// it, the hand shrinking while the grip remained constant.

This grip //fits//. My fingers find their positions naturally. The leather seats against a palm that is smoother, softer, but no less capable of holding tension.

<<speech "yseldra">>Nock an arrow.<</speech>>

She produces a quiver from the weapons rack and hands it to me. I sling it over my shoulder, the strap crossing my chest diagonally, running between my breasts, pressing the corset flat against the left cup, and pull an arrow.

I nock it. Set my feet in the old wide stance, the one drilled into me since the first day I picked up a bow at thirteen. I draw.

The string comes back. Fifty pounds is lighter, noticeably, almost embarrassingly so. Where seventy required the old shoulders to //work//, to brace and lock and engage every fibre of deltoid and trapezius, fifty comes smoothly. The muscles engage but do not strain. The draw feels... manageable.

I anchor. Two fingers at the corner of my jaw. String brushing the chin. Arm extended in a clean line from elbow through shaft to target.

The form is perfect. Textbook. The form that won me three harvest tournament ribbons.

The string crosses my chest.

[[The bowstring|warrior_queen_day_4_6]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_6

Pain //detonates// through my left breast like a heated brand pressed to the skin.

The bowstring, on its path from hand to jaw, has dragged directly across the swell of the left breast, not the flat, ignorable chest of the prince I had been, but flesh that is budding and nerve-rich and exquisitely, //unforgivingly// tender, even through the training corset's padding. The string has caught the outermost curve of the cup, compressed it inward, and scraped across the tissue beneath with a friction that translates through lambskin and linen into pure, crystalline agony.

I yelp.

The sound that leaves my throat is, I will deny it to my dying breath, but the truth must be recorded somewhere, a //squeak//. Not a grunt, not a bark, not the masculine exclamation of pain the prince would have produced. A squeak. High, sharp, involuntary, and pitched in a register the prince's vocal cords had never visited and that my current vocal cords are visiting with increasing frequency.

The arrow looses wild. It sails high over the target, over the //yard//, and buries itself in a wooden fence post thirty yards beyond with a distant, mocking //thunk// that echoes off the castle walls like laughter.

I lower the bow. Breathe through my teeth. Breathe //hard//, because the left breast is on fire. Not the tender ache of growth, not the compression-throb of the corset, but a sharp, electric, //specific// agony that radiates outward from the point of impact in pulsing waves.

Even through the corset, through the cups, through the padding, through the lambskin lining designed expressly to protect the tissue, the bowstring has found its target with a precision that would be admirable if it were not agonising.

I press my hand against the left breast. Gently. Through the leather tunic. Through the corset. Through the layers of protection that have proven, in this singular case, entirely insufficient. The flesh beneath is //hot//. Swollen further in protest, and the nipple, buried behind layers of fabric and padding, sends a bright, continuous distress signal that runs from the tip of the breast through the ribcage and directly into the pain centres of my brain.

<<speech "mc">>Gods. Nnhh.<</speech>>

The curse comes out as a hiss. I clench my jaw. The breast throbs.

[[Yseldra's response|warrior_queen_day_4_7]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_7

Yseldra does not laugh. She does not smirk. She does, however, allow herself a single dry exhalation through her nose that might be a distant relative of amusement, visiting from distant provinces and unsure of the local customs.

<<speech "yseldra">>Every girl learns this lesson.<</speech>>

She says it flatly. Without sympathy. Without cruelty. With the precise neutrality of a woman stating a fact that is, to her, as evident as gravity.

<<speech "yseldra">>Most learn it at thirteen, when the buds first appear and they reach for their first bow without thinking. The string finds the breast. The breast objects. The girl learns. You are late to the lesson, but the lesson is the same.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You might have //warned// me.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>I could have described the sensation in exacting detail and you would have nodded and drawn exactly as you did, because men, and those who were recently men, do not believe warnings about breast pain until the breast produces pain. It is a universal constant. The warning would have wasted both our time. The bruise will not.<</speech>>

She is, infuriatingly, correct.

<<speech "yseldra">>Lower the bow. Face me.<</speech>>

I lower the bow. Face her. The left breast throbs in rhythm with my heartbeat, a deep, pulsing ache I can feel radiating outward from the impact site, through the cup, through the corset, through the layers of my awareness. The welt from the string will be forming now, beneath all that protection. A thin line of angry red across the curve of new flesh. A line that will ache for days.

Yseldra steps behind me. Without warning or preamble, she seizes my draw arm, the right, and //wrenches// it upward.

<<speech "mc">>Ow, that hurts.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>The elbow. Higher. Here.<</speech>>

She physically repositions the arm. The adjustment is precise, unyielding, and entirely without sympathy. She angles the forearm so the bowstring's path will arc //above// the breast line rather than across it. The elbow rises to the height of my ear. The draw arm twists into a position that feels like someone has rearranged my skeleton while I was not looking.

<<speech "yseldra">>Your anchor point is wrong. The jaw corner was correct for a flat chest. You no longer have a flat chest. The new anchor is here...<</speech>>

She presses her fingertip against my cheekbone. Hard. The contact is a statement, not a caress.

<<speech "yseldra">>...the cheekbone. Higher anchor means higher string path. Higher string path means the string passes above the breast instead of across it. You will lose your old sight picture. You will lose your old aiming instincts. Everything you know about shooting is now wrong. Welcome to archery with breasts.<</speech>>

[[Learn the feminine draw|warrior_queen_day_4_8]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_8

The feminine draw feels like trying to write with my feet whilst standing on my head in a stiff wind.

Everything is //wrong//. The elbow position, high, tucked close to the skull, strains muscles in the shoulder the old draw never troubled. The shoulder itself is narrower than it was, the deltoid thinner, and the new position demands engagement of the trapezius in a way that immediately begins to burn with a deep, structural ache.

The cheekbone anchor is unstable. The jaw corner had provided a solid, bony shelf for the string hand, two fingers seated against bone, reliable, unchanging. The cheekbone is higher, shallower, and the angle of approach means the string presses against skin that is, I notice with a start, //softer// than it was. The cheekbone itself is higher now, more prominent, the facial structure shifting beneath Thalara's potions into something with less jaw and more cheek. The anchor that had been bone against finger is becoming bone against softer finger against softer skin. And the sight picture. Gods, the sight picture.

My old aim was calibrated over seven years and approximately ten thousand arrows. The jaw anchor and level elbow produced a specific line from eye through shaft to target that is burned into my nervous system like a brand. The cheekbone anchor moves everything, the angle of the shaft, the trajectory of the arrow, the relationship between eye and target. I am not merely learning a new anchor. I am //unlearning// an entire ballistic system and replacing it from scratch.

I draw. Aim. Release.

The arrow hits the wall. Not the target, not the target frame, not the hay bales behind the target. The //wall//. Four feet above the target and two feet to the left, burying itself in the mortar between two stones with a //crack// that sends chips of masonry showering onto the cobblestones.

<<speech "yseldra">>Again.<</speech>>

I draw. The string hums above my left breast, I can feel it hovering there, a hair's breadth of clearance between taut bowstring and tender flesh, the proximity generating a low thrum of anxiety in the tissue that has so recently been introduced to the string's displeasure.

I aim. Release.

The arrow hits the hay bale. Not the target painted upon it, but the bale itself. A marginal improvement.

<<speech "yseldra">>Again.<</speech>>

[[Drilling the draw|warrior_queen_day_4_9]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_9

We drill for two hours.

Yseldra is, and I say this with the cautious respect one affords a natural disaster, //relentless//. She makes Aldric look like a kindly uncle distributing sweets on feast days. Where Aldric corrects through laconic instruction, Yseldra corrects through physical adjustment, seizing my elbow, wrenching my shoulder, pressing my spine into alignment with a palm between the shoulder blades that brooks no argument.

Her hands are impersonal. Efficient. The hands of a warrior adjusting a weapon, not touching a body. But the body she is adjusting is a body in transition, and every point of contact reminds me of things I cannot ignore.

When she presses my shoulder back, the motion pulls the corset tight across my chest, and the breast tissue compresses against the cups with a sensation that is half ache, half warmth.

When she adjusts my stance, feet narrower, weight dropped into the hips, the feminine guard adapted for archery, her boot nudges my trailing foot, and I am aware of the way my hips //accept// the narrower stance more readily than they accepted the old wide one. The body knows what it wants to stand like. The mind is still arguing.

When she repositions my draw arm for the fourteenth time, her fingers brush the quiver strap crossing my chest, and the strap shifts against the corset, and the corset shifts against the breasts, and I feel a chain reaction of tiny adjustments ripple through the tissue, each breast resettling in its cup, the flesh conforming to the new angle like water conforming to a tilted glass.

By the end of the second hour, I am hitting the target.

Not the centre. Not with any consistency. But the arrows are landing within the painted rings, third ring, fourth ring, the occasional glorious second-ring strike that makes Yseldra's mouth twitch by approximately one millimetre.

My shoulder is screaming. The higher elbow position has engaged muscles that spent twenty years in comfortable retirement, and they are not pleased about the conscription. The trapezius on the right side is locked in a cramp that sends roots of pain down through my shoulder blade and into the ribs. The deltoid, thinner, weaker, the muscle mass slowly dissolving under the potions' patient alchemy, trembles with each draw.

But the breast is safe. The string arcs cleanly above the left cup, clearing the tissue by an inch, more clearance than strictly necessary, but I am //generous// with that inch. The memory of the impact is too fresh, the welt still throbbing beneath the padding, and I would rather miss every target in the yard than feel that brand-hot agony again.

<<speech "yseldra">>Halt there.<</speech>>

I lower the bow. My arms drop to my sides with the limp gratitude of muscles held at tension for too long.

Yseldra studies me. Her ice-blue eyes catalogue the sweat, the trembling, the loosened braid, the way I am unconsciously pressing my left arm against my chest to guard the injured breast.

<<speech "yseldra">>The draw improves. The aim is atrocious. But you are not hitting yourself, which is progress.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>High praise.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>The highest I intend to offer. Sit. Drink, and when you have caught your breath, we will discuss equipment.<</speech>>

[[Water break and equipment|warrior_queen_day_4_10]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_10

I sit on the weapons rack, the same rack Aldric perches upon, which has apparently become the designated location for instructors to deliver uncomfortable truths, and drink from the water jug. The water is cold and tastes like the stones of the cistern. I drink until my stomach sloshes.

Yseldra remains standing. She has not sat down once during the entire session. I am beginning to suspect the woman might not actually possess the ability to sit, as though the concept had been omitted from her construction.

<<speech "yseldra">>You need a breast guard.<</speech>>

The statement lands in the Iron Garden with the force of a practice blade to the ribs. I nearly choke on the water.

<<speech "mc">>I... what did you say?<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>A breast guard. Boiled leather, shaped to cup and compress. Worn beneath the tunic for every draw until the day you die or cease to shoot, whichever comes first.<</speech>>

She says it the way one might describe the necessity of stringing a bow before using it, as an obvious, mechanical requirement that only a fool would question.

<<speech "yseldra">>The corset provides adequate support for swordwork, but archery requires specific protection. The string's path, even with the feminine draw, will graze the breast on occasion, during high wind, during rapid shooting, during the hundred small misalignments that occur when you are tired or frightened or shooting at something that is trying to kill you. The breast guard eliminates the risk. It flattens and shields the tissue against the string's contact.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I have only been... I mean, they are not large enough to require...<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>They are large enough. If the string found them today through a training corset, they are large enough, and they will be larger tomorrow, and the day after, and the month after that. The guard is not negotiable. I shall speak to the armourer myself.<</speech>>

She pauses and then, with a precision that suggests the information is so obvious it barely warrants breath, says,

<<speech "yseldra">>There is no shame in the wearing. Every woman archer in every army in every kingdom upon this earth wears one. It is no different than a codpiece, save the location.<</speech>>

The comparison startles a laugh from me.

A real laugh. Not the bitter, sharp exhalation that has served as laughter these past weeks, but a genuine sound, warm and unburdened and //surprised//. It rises from the belly, exactly where Aldric taught me to breathe, and emerges from a throat that shapes it differently than it did a month ago. Higher. Lighter. Breathier. Not a man's laugh and not quite a woman's, but something in between, something that is becoming its own thing, as all of me is becoming its own thing.

Yseldra's mouth twitches. Just barely. The barest ghost of warmth crosses a landscape that is otherwise Arctic tundra in expression. Then the ice returns and seals her features shut.

<<speech "yseldra">>Do not mistake that for fondness. On the morrow I expect every arrow upon the target. Miss, and you shall run the battlements in full plate until you vomit. Now...<</speech>>

She nods toward the archery range.

<<speech "yseldra">>Twenty more. The drill is the same, and this time, I want the breath held at the top of the draw. Your chest is moving when you release, the expansion of the ribcage shifts the string path. Hold the breath. Still the chest. The breasts must not move during the release. If they move, the arrow moves. If the arrow moves, someone dies who should not have.<</speech>>

[[Twenty more arrows|warrior_queen_day_4_11]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_11

Twenty more arrows. I shoot them with my jaw clenched and my breath held at the top of each draw, the way Yseldra demands.

She is right about the breathing. The chest-breath I used my entire shooting life expanded the ribcage, which shifted the corset, which shifted the breast tissue, which shifted the string path by millimetres that translate to inches at thirty yards and feet at a hundred. The belly-breath Aldric taught me, the same breath that anchors the feminine guard, keeps the chest //still//. The ribcage holds its shape. The breasts hold their position within the cups. The string holds its line.

Arrow eleven hits the second ring.

Arrow fifteen hits the first ring.

Arrow nineteen hits the centre.

I stare at the target. The arrow protrudes from the painted bullseye, dead centre, the shaft still vibrating with the force of impact. The fletching is straight. The angle is true. A clean, undeniable strike.

From the cheekbone anchor. From the feminine draw. From a body with breasts and wider hips and a lower centre of gravity and a breath that lives in the belly instead of the chest.

<<speech "yseldra">>Acceptable.<</speech>>

From Yseldra, that word carries the same weight as Aldric's nod, currency hoarded and spent with a miser's calculation, worth more than any tournament ribbon I have ever won.

She walks to the target and withdraws the arrow. Examines its angle of entry with the critical attention of a jeweller examining a cut.

<<speech "yseldra">>Your old draw would have hit this target nine times in ten. Your new draw hits it once in twenty. But the once is clean. The form is there. The body has the potential. What it lacks is repetition.<</speech>>

She returns the arrow to my quiver. Her hand brushes my shoulder in the motion, accidental, perhaps, but the contact sparks awareness. Her armour is cold against my skin, even through the leather. The touch lasts less than a second. But I feel it radiate through the shoulder, down through the chest, as though the breast tissue were now a secondary nervous system running in parallel to the primary one, picking up signals the old body would have missed entirely.

<<speech "yseldra">>The armourer shall have the breast guard by tomorrow. Have it fitted before you come to the range. Tight. Snug. The leather should cup and compress, not crush, not flatten, but //hold//. You want the tissue immobilised during the draw, not punished for existing.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Not punished for existing. I shall try to remember that principle generally.<</speech>>

Yseldra looks at me. The ice-blue eyes thaw by precisely one degree.

<<speech "yseldra">>That principle applies to more than the breast guard, Your Highness. Remember it.<</speech>>

She turns and departs. Her armour does not clank. It //whispers//, oiled plate moving over oiled plate, each piece fitted to her body with the same relentless precision she brings to everything. She walks like a weapon that has been stored properly and is merely relocating to a different armoury.

I stand at the archery range alone, the bow hanging from my aching hand, and feel the left breast throb beneath the corset. The welt from the first draw is still there, still hot, still angry, still announcing itself with each heartbeat. I will need to examine it tonight. Apply the salve. Let Maera's careful fingers tend the damage.

The thought of Maera's fingers on the welt, on //that// spot, on the curve where the string found the breast, sends a ripple of sensation through the tissue that I choose not to examine too closely.

[[Return to the corridor|warrior_queen_day_4_12]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_12

The corridor from the Iron Garden to the main castle is cool and dim. Arrow-slit windows throw bars of light across the flagstones. My boots, slightly loose now, the feet having narrowed, echo with each step. I pass the entrance to the Great Library and pause.

Through the heavy door, half-ajar, I can hear the scratch of a quill. A throat clears. The rustle of parchment.

Jeral.

I push the door open. The library's vast interior stretches before me, shelves climbing three storeys, ladders and catwalks and the dusty, breathing hush of ten thousand books existing in quiet communion. Jeral sits at his customary desk near the window, surrounded by scrolls, his spectacles perched on the very tip of his thin nose in a position that defies both gravity and good sense. He looks up.

<<speech "jeral">>Your Highness. You appear to have been shot.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Nearly. By myself, in a manner of speaking.<</speech>>

I lean against the doorframe. My arms ache. My shoulder burns. The breast throbs. But there is comfort in Jeral's presence, the man is a pool of calm in a castle of storms. Nothing rattles him. Nothing surprises him. He exists in a state of perpetual, gentle expectation that everything will be exactly as complicated as it always is, and his only task is to record the complications accurately.

<<speech "jeral">>Dame Yseldra, I presume. I noted her arrival this morning. She is not a woman who enters a building quietly, despite all evidence suggesting she believes she does.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>She taught me the feminine draw. The archery form for... for someone with...<</speech>>

I gesture vaguely at my chest. Jeral's eyes do not flicker. Do not drop. Do not acknowledge the gesture with anything other than an infinitesimal adjustment of his spectacles.

<<speech "jeral">>For someone whose anatomy has changed in ways that affect the string's path. Yes. I have read about the feminine draw in Lady Orenthia's //Treatise on the Queen's Blades//. Third floor, east alcove, second shelf from the top. Published three hundred years ago and largely ignored since, which is the fate of most texts written by women about women's combat techniques. Dame Yseldra is one of perhaps four people in the kingdom who still practice it.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You knew she was coming.<</speech>>

<<speech "jeral">>I knew Sir Aldric had requested her presence through the duty roster, which I maintain. I drew the obvious inference. It is not difficult when one pays attention, which most people decline to do on principle.<</speech>>

He sets down his quill. Folds his hands. The gesture is precise, symmetrical, Jeral's equivalent of opening a door and inviting you in.

<<speech "jeral">>How did you find it? The new draw?<</speech>>

